WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.923
In ASP.

2
00:00:00.923 --> 00:00:06.229
NET Core, trust boundaries show up wherever request data enters the application.

3
00:00:06.229 --> 00:00:16.148
Request bodies, query strings, route parameters, headers, cookies, JSON payloads, XML payloads, and form posts all arrive from a less trusted context.

4
00:00:16.148 --> 00:00:22.684
Even when framework features bind those values into objects, the original source is still external.

5
00:00:22.684 --> 00:00:32.604
The application must decide which fields are allowed, which formats are acceptable, and which values are meaningful for the operation being performed.

6
00:00:32.604 --> 00:00:35.526
File uploads deserve a boundary of their own.

7
00:00:35.526 --> 00:00:43.369
The file name, extension, declared content type, size, metadata, and content may all be user-controlled or misleading.

8
00:00:43.369 --> 00:00:56.211
A safe design does not trust the original filename as a storage path, does not treat an extension as proof of content, and does not allow a large or malformed upload to consume resources without limits.

9
00:00:56.211 --> 00:01:02.670
Validation should happen before storage, processing, scanning, or business workflow decisions.

10
00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:06.746
Server-side and background systems have less obvious inputs.

11
00:01:06.746 --> 00:01:16.511
Environment variables and configuration files often arrive as strings and may be missing, malformed, or set for a different deployment environment.

12
00:01:16.511 --> 00:01:20.049
Message queues can carry stale or malformed messages.

13
00:01:20.049 --> 00:01:25.431
Database records may contain legacy values that no longer match the current model.

14
00:01:25.431 --> 00:01:29.507
Third-party API responses can drift after an upstream change.

15
00:01:29.507 --> 00:01:32.044
Internal does not always mean trusted.

16
00:01:32.044 --> 00:01:38.042
A useful review habit is to list where data crosses a system, process, or ownership boundary.

17
00:01:38.042 --> 00:01:44.348
For each boundary, ask what the receiving code assumes and where those assumptions are enforced.

18
00:01:44.348 --> 00:01:56.190
The answer should be visible in code: DTOs for external contracts, validators for accepted values, parsing routines for conversions, and service-layer rules for business meaning.

19
00:01:56.190 --> 00:02:00.880
Clear boundaries make validation maintainable instead of accidental.
